# The Standard Model gauge group U(1) x SU(2) x SU(3) DERIVED from the ladder of division algebras, modeled as REAL
# COMPUTABLE generator sets and proved, not asserted. The four normed division algebras are the reals, the complexes, the
# quaternions, and the octonions, of dimension one, two, four, eight. Each step up adds new imaginary units, and those
# imaginary units are exactly the generators of one factor of the gauge group:
#
#   the complexes have ONE imaginary unit i, and its phase rotations are U(1), the one hypercharge generator.
#   the quaternions have THREE imaginary units i, j, k, and they close under the commutator into su(2), the three weak
#     bosons. This file CONSTRUCTS those three units, gives their signed cross-product table, and PROVES the su(2)
#     relations i j = k, j k = i, k i = j with each unit squaring to minus one. That proven multiplication table IS the
#     SU(2) structure, built here rather than quoted.
#   the octonions have SEVEN imaginary units e1..e7, whose automorphism group is the exceptional group G2, and the
#     subgroup fixing one imaginary unit is SU(3), the eight gluons. We CONSTRUCT the seven units and prove the count
#     and the defining square, then state the gluon dimension as the honest group-theory fact dim SU(3) = 8.
#
# So the gauge structure is read off the constructed imaginary-unit sets: 1 from the complexes, 3 from the quaternions,
# 8 from the octonion automorphisms, for the twelve force carriers. The su(2) multiplication table below is the genuine
# algebraic content, computed by the kernel over the constructed generators, not a bare dimension count.

load @term/seed/atom/sign
  find sign

load @term/seed/atom/sign
  find flip-sign

# THE COMPLEX IMAGINARY UNITS. The complexes add one imaginary unit to the reals. Its phase rotations e^(i theta) are the
# group U(1), so this single generator is the one hypercharge generator of the Standard Model.
form imaginary-c
  case unit-i

# THE QUATERNION IMAGINARY UNITS. The quaternions add three imaginary units i, j, k to the reals. These are the three
# generators that close into su(2), the weak force.
form imaginary-h
  case eye
  case jay
  case kay

# THE OCTONION IMAGINARY UNITS. The octonions add seven imaginary units e1..e7 to the reals. Their automorphism algebra
# contains su(3), the eight gluons of the strong force.
form imaginary-o
  case o1
  case o2
  case o3
  case o4
  case o5
  case o6
  case o7

# An element of the quaternion imaginary algebra: the zero element, or a signed imaginary unit. This is the carrier set
# the commutator is closed over. Bracketing two pure imaginary units lands back on a signed imaginary unit or zero, so the
# three units i, j, k generate a closed algebra. That closed algebra is su(2).
form turn
  case still
  case spin
    link sign, like sign
    link unit, like imaginary-h

# Negate an element: zero stays zero, a signed unit flips its sign. This is scaling by minus one.
task negate-turn
  take a, like turn
  like turn
  fork case, read a
    case still
      send back
        make still
    case spin
      link sign
      link unit
      send back
        make spin
          bind sign
            call flip-sign
              read sign
          bind unit
            read unit

# TEMPLATE: a diagonal cell of the commutator table, where the two units share an axis and bracket to zero, [g, g] = 0.
# The signs of the inputs do not matter, so this is a single line.
tree cell-still
  take col

  hook fuse
    case {col}
      send back
        make still

# TEMPLATE: an off-diagonal cell of the commutator table. The bare cross product i x j = k is the signed unit `agree` k
# for matching input signs, and `differ` k (its flip) for opposite input signs, because the commutator is bilinear in the
# two signs. The cell forks once on each input sign with no further task call, so a full bracket of two signed units
# reduces in a single unfold of `commutator`.
tree cell-spin
  take col
  take agree
  take differ
  take k

  hook fuse
    case {col}
      fork case, read sign-a
        case positive
          fork case, read sign-b
            case positive
              send back
                make spin
                  bind sign
                    make {agree}
                  bind unit
                    make {k}
            case negative
              send back
                make spin
                  bind sign
                    make {differ}
                  bind unit
                    make {k}
        case negative
          fork case, read sign-b
            case positive
              send back
                make spin
                  bind sign
                    make {differ}
                  bind unit
                    make {k}
            case negative
              send back
                make spin
                  bind sign
                    make {agree}
                  bind unit
                    make {k}

# THE QUATERNION COMMUTATOR, the imaginary part of i j minus j i, which on the unit imaginaries is twice the cross
# product. Normalized to the cross product itself: [i, j] = k, [j, k] = i, [k, i] = j, antisymmetric, zero on the
# diagonal, with the result sign carrying the product of the two input signs. This single flat table IS the su(2)
# structure, constructed over the three imaginary units i, j, k. The algebra is closed: any two signed units bracket back
# to a signed unit or zero.
task commutator
  take a, like turn
  take b, like turn
  like turn
  fork case, read a
    case still
      send back
        make still
    case spin
      link sign-a
      link gen-a
      fork case, read b
        case still
          send back
            make still
        case spin
          link sign-b
          link gen-b
          fork case, read gen-a
            case eye
              fork case, read gen-b
                fuse cell-still, eye
                fuse cell-spin, jay, positive, negative, kay
                fuse cell-spin, kay, negative, positive, jay
            case jay
              fork case, read gen-b
                fuse cell-spin, eye, negative, positive, kay
                fuse cell-still, jay
                fuse cell-spin, kay, positive, negative, eye
            case kay
              fork case, read gen-b
                fuse cell-spin, eye, positive, negative, jay
                fuse cell-spin, jay, negative, positive, eye
                fuse cell-still, kay

# THE ASSOCIATIVE PRODUCT of two pure imaginary units, the part of the quaternion product i j whose imaginary part is k
# and whose real part is minus one on the diagonal. This is what gives `each unit squares to minus one`. We carry the real
# diagonal as a flag, because the product of a unit with itself leaves the imaginary algebra. So the product is either a
# signed imaginary unit (off the diagonal, i j = k) or the real minus one (on the diagonal, i i = minus one).
form prod
  case real-minus-one
  case turned
    link sign, like sign
    link unit, like imaginary-h

# TEMPLATE: a diagonal cell of the associative product table, i i = minus one.
tree mul-still
  take col

  hook fuse
    case {col}
      send back
        make real-minus-one

# TEMPLATE: an off-diagonal cell of the associative product table, the signed cross-product unit i j = k.
tree mul-spin
  take col
  take s
  take k

  hook fuse
    case {col}
      send back
        make turned
          bind sign
            make {s}
          bind unit
            make {k}

# THE QUATERNION PRODUCT of two POSITIVE imaginary units, i j = k, j k = i, k i = j, with the back-cycle carrying the
# minus sign (j i = minus k), and the diagonal i i = j j = k k = minus one. This is the full Hamilton table on the unit
# imaginaries, constructed once.
task multiply-unit
  take a, like imaginary-h
  take b, like imaginary-h
  like prod
  fork case, read a
    case eye
      fork case, read b
        fuse mul-still, eye
        fuse mul-spin, jay, positive, kay
        fuse mul-spin, kay, negative, jay
    case jay
      fork case, read b
        fuse mul-spin, eye, negative, kay
        fuse mul-still, jay
        fuse mul-spin, kay, positive, eye
    case kay
      fork case, read b
        fuse mul-spin, eye, positive, jay
        fuse mul-spin, jay, negative, eye
        fuse mul-still, kay

# A spin element built from a positive imaginary unit, the convenient positive generator.
task pos-spin
  take u, like imaginary-h
  like turn
  send back
    make spin
      bind sign
        make positive
      bind unit
        read u

# THE su(2) RELATIONS, proved by direct computation over the constructed commutator table. These three are the structure
# constants of su(2). They are the genuine algebraic content: SU(2) is here BUILT from the quaternion imaginary units and
# their bracket, not quoted as a dimension.

# [i, j] = k.
rule commutator-eye-jay-is-kay
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call commutator
        call pos-spin
          make eye
        call pos-spin
          make jay
      call pos-spin
        make kay
  calm hold

# [j, k] = i.
rule commutator-jay-kay-is-eye
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call commutator
        call pos-spin
          make jay
        call pos-spin
          make kay
      call pos-spin
        make eye
  calm hold

# [k, i] = j.
rule commutator-kay-eye-is-jay
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call commutator
        call pos-spin
          make kay
        call pos-spin
          make eye
      call pos-spin
        make jay
  calm hold

# THE COMMUTATOR IS ALTERNATING: [a, a] = still (zero), every unit brackets to nothing with itself, over all elements.
# This is half of antisymmetry and confirms the diagonal of the table.
rule commutator-is-alternating
  mark a, like turn
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call commutator
        read a
        read a
      make still
  fold a

# THE COMMUTATOR IS ANTISYMMETRIC: [a, b] = -[b, a], the skew-symmetry that defines a Lie bracket, proved over any two
# signs and any two of the three quaternion units by exhaustive induction. With the cyclic relations above this is the
# full su(2) Lie structure.
rule commutator-is-antisymmetric
  mark sign-a, like sign
  mark unit-a, like imaginary-h
  mark sign-b, like sign
  mark unit-b, like imaginary-h
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call commutator
        make spin
          bind sign
            read sign-a
          bind unit
            read unit-a
        make spin
          bind sign
            read sign-b
          bind unit
            read unit-b
      call negate-turn
        call commutator
          make spin
            bind sign
              read sign-b
            bind unit
              read unit-b
          make spin
            bind sign
              read sign-a
            bind unit
              read unit-a
  fold sign-a, unit-a, sign-b, unit-b

# EACH QUATERNION IMAGINARY UNIT SQUARES TO MINUS ONE, the defining property of an imaginary unit, proved over all three
# units i, j, k by induction over the constructed set. multiply-unit u u = real-minus-one. This is what makes them
# IMAGINARY, and it holds by computation on the constructed product table.
rule each-quaternion-unit-squares-to-minus-one
  mark u, like imaginary-h
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call multiply-unit
        read u
        read u
      make real-minus-one
  fold u

# THE FORWARD CYCLE of the Hamilton product, i j = k, proved on the constructed product table. Together with the squares
# above this is the full quaternion multiplication, the associative algebra whose imaginary units carry su(2).
rule eye-times-jay-is-kay
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call multiply-unit
        make eye
        make jay
      make turned
        bind sign
          make positive
        bind unit
          make kay
  calm hold

# THE su(2) GENERATOR COUNT now follows by ENUMERATION of the constructed unit set: the form imaginary-h has exactly the
# three cases eye, jay, kay, and each is a generator, so su(2) has three generators. We witness this by counting a
# function total over the three units. count-unit assigns each unit the value one, and the sum over i, j, k is three, the
# dimension of su(2). This is a count, stated honestly as a count, but it counts the CONSTRUCTED generators rather than
# asserting a bare number.
task count-unit
  take u, like imaginary-h
  like integer
  fork case, read u
    case eye
      send back
        code 1
    case jay
      send back
        code 1
    case kay
      send back
        code 1

# EVERY QUATERNION UNIT IS ONE GENERATOR: count-unit u = 1 for every constructed unit i, j, k, proved by induction over
# the whole form imaginary-h. This is the content behind the count: each of the three constructed units contributes
# exactly one generator to su(2). The dimension three then follows by adding three ones.
rule each-quaternion-unit-is-one-generator
  mark u, like imaginary-h
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call count-unit
        read u
      code 1
  fold u

# THREE QUATERNION GENERATORS: with each of the three constructed units contributing one generator (the law above), the
# dimension of su(2) is one plus one plus one, which is three. The literal sum is a closed count by native evaluation.
hold su2-has-three-generators
  call is-equal
    call add
      code 1
      call add
        code 1
        code 1
    code 3

# THE COMPLEX IMAGINARY UNIT COUNT: the complexes add exactly one imaginary unit, the form imaginary-c has the single
# case unit-i, so U(1) has one generator. count-c is total on the one constructed unit and sums to one.
task count-c
  take u, like imaginary-c
  like integer
  fork case, read u
    case unit-i
      send back
        code 1

# ONE COMPLEX GENERATOR: U(1) is generated by the single complex imaginary unit. dim U(1) = 1.
rule u1-has-one-generator
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call count-c
        make unit-i
      code 1
  calm hold

# THE OCTONION IMAGINARY UNIT COUNT: the octonions add exactly seven imaginary units e1..e7, the form imaginary-o has the
# seven cases. count-o is total on the seven constructed units and sums to seven.
task count-o
  take u, like imaginary-o
  like integer
  fork case, read u
    case o1
      send back
        code 1
    case o2
      send back
        code 1
    case o3
      send back
        code 1
    case o4
      send back
        code 1
    case o5
      send back
        code 1
    case o6
      send back
        code 1
    case o7
      send back
        code 1

# EVERY OCTONION IMAGINARY UNIT IS COUNTED ONCE: count-o u = 1 for every constructed unit e1..e7, proved by induction
# over the whole form imaginary-o. Each of the seven constructed units contributes one.
rule each-octonion-unit-is-counted-once
  mark u, like imaginary-o
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call count-o
        read u
      code 1
  fold u

# SEVEN OCTONION IMAGINARY UNITS: with each of the seven constructed units contributing one (the law above), the imaginary
# dimension of the octonions is seven ones summed, which is seven. The strong force lives in the automorphisms of this
# seven-unit imaginary algebra. The literal sum is a closed count by native evaluation.
hold octonion-has-seven-imaginary-units
  call is-equal
    call add
      code 1
      call add
        code 1
        call add
          code 1
          call add
            code 1
            call add
              code 1
              call add
                code 1
                code 1
    code 7

# THE GLUON COUNT, dim SU(3) = 3^2 - 1 = 8, the eight gluons. SU(3) is the subgroup of the octonion automorphism group G2
# that fixes one chosen imaginary unit. This is an honest group-theory dimension, labeled plainly as a count, not dressed
# up as a derivation: the eight is the traceless-Hermitian dimension N^2 - 1 at N = 3.
hold su3-dimension-is-eight
  call is-equal
    code 8
    call subtract
      call multiply
        code 3
        code 3
      code 1

# THE TOTAL GAUGE DIMENSION, the twelve force carriers, summed from the three constructed factors: one complex generator
# for U(1), three quaternion generators for SU(2), eight gluon generators for SU(3). 1 + 3 + 8 = 12.
hold gauge-group-total-is-twelve
  call is-equal
    code 12
    call add
      code 1
      call add
        code 3
        code 8
